(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({
n: 16, hours: 6, title: "After the Bid: Contracts, Billing and Growing the Division",
slides: [

{ t: "cover",
  h: "After the Bid: Contracts, Billing and Growing the Division",
  lead: "From the award letter to the final retention check, then using every finished job to price the next one and grow the division.",
  objectives: [
    "Mark up a subcontract and send a one-page rider with the changes that matter",
    "Apply California rules on pay-if-paid, prompt payment, retention and indemnity",
    "Build submittals, a schedule of values and pay applications with change orders and retention",
    "Protect payment with preliminary notices, lien and stop payment deadlines and the right releases",
    "Run change orders, T&M tickets, plant establishment and closeout without losing money",
    "Feed job cost back into pricing; track KPIs; plan hiring, bonding and growth"
  ],
  time: [["Reading and slides", "1.75 h"], ["Practice assignments", "4.25 h"], ["Module total", "6 h"]],
  body: "<p>The legal points are a starting map, not legal advice. Have a construction attorney review your standard subcontract markups once. Sample clauses are made up for teaching; real subcontracts vary.</p>"
},

{ h: "From “you're low” to work on site",
  lead: "Being low is not a contract: order nothing until a subcontract is signed or you hold written authorization.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Verbal or email “you're low”</td><td>Not a contract. No orders.</td></tr>
<tr><td>LOI (letter of intent)</td><td>GC plans to subcontract with you; may authorize limited work such as submittals. Read what it covers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notice of award (public)</td><td>Agency awards the prime. On the City's K-24-1952, the City prepared contract documents about 21 days after opening, then awarded once the signed contract, bonds and insurance arrived.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Subcontract signed</strong></td><td>Now you have a deal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bonds, COI (certificate of insurance) and endorsements to the GC</td><td>Before you go on site</td></tr>
<tr><td>Submittals, precon, work</td><td></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Asked to start before signing? Get written authorization stating the work, the price and that the subcontract terms will follow.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego Bidding Documents K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, Instructions to Bidders item 8."
},

{ h: "Subcontract review and your rider",
  lead: "GC subcontracts protect the GC; check every clause, then send a one-page rider with your standard asks.",
  exLabel: "Rider template (adjust with your attorney)",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Clause</th><th>Check</th><th>Rider request</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Same sheets, spec sections, addenda, inclusions and exclusions as your proposal</td><td>“Subcontractor's Proposal #[ ] dated [ ], including exclusions and clarifications, is Exhibit B and controls scope.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payment</td><td>Billing date, pay timing, pay-if-paid</td><td>“Within 7 days after Contractor receives payment for Subcontractor's work.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retention</td><td>Percent and release trigger</td><td>“Not to exceed 5%, released within 30 days after acceptance of Subcontractor's work.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lien releases</td><td>Unconditional releases demanded with billing</td><td>“Conditional statutory releases with each billing; unconditional only after payment clears.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notice and changes</td><td>Claim deadline, who approves, markup cap</td><td>7 calendar days' notice; “signed T&amp;M tickets are written authorization; Exhibit B unit prices apply.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule</td><td>Start, finish, LDs passed down, no damages for delay</td><td>Duration from turnover of ready areas; LDs only to the extent you caused them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warranty and establishment</td><td>Start date; meaning of “acceptance”</td><td>“Start at acceptance of Subcontractor's work.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indemnity</td><td>Whose fault you pay for</td><td>“To the extent caused by Subcontractor's negligence or willful misconduct.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurance and bonds</td><td>Limits, endorsements, sub bonds</td><td>Price extra endorsements; price bonds if the invitation required them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disputes; flow-down</td><td>Venue; “bound to Contractor as Contractor is bound to Owner”</td><td>Mediation first, San Diego County venue; a copy of the prime contract before signing</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Log what each GC accepts. On its next invitation, the remaining risk goes into your price or your go/no-go (<a href="#m3-1">Module 3</a>). Top five to ask every time: scope exhibit, pay-if-paid, retention, unconditional waivers, notice deadlines.</p>`
},

{ h: "Sample clauses · scope, payment, retention, waivers",
  lead: "Translate each phrase into what it does to you, then ask for the specific fix.",
  exLabel: "Sample clauses (made up for teaching)",
  ex: `<p>“Subcontractor shall furnish all labor, materials, equipment and services necessary for the complete landscape and irrigation work, as shown or reasonably inferable from the Contract Documents, including all work customarily performed by the landscape trade. Subcontractor is bound to Contractor by the terms of the Prime Contract to the same extent Contractor is bound to Owner.”</p>
<p>“Payment to Subcontractor is expressly conditioned on Contractor's receipt of payment from Owner. Contractor shall retain 10% of each progress payment until final completion and acceptance of the Project. With each payment application, Subcontractor shall deliver an unconditional waiver and release covering all work through the billing date.”</p>`,
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Phrase</th><th>Effect</th><th>Ask / law</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>“complete landscape and irrigation work”</td><td>Everything, including your exclusions</td><td>“Work per Proposal #26-041 Rev. 1, Exhibit B, including its exclusions and clarifications.” At minimum attach the exclusions page.</td></tr>
<tr><td>“reasonably inferable”, “customarily performed”</td><td>Unshown work arguably expected</td><td>Delete, or limit to items needed for your listed scope</td></tr>
<tr><td>“bound… to the same extent” (flow-down)</td><td>Prime contract terms apply to you</td><td>Prime contract copy first; your proposal controls conflicts</td></tr>
<tr><td>“expressly conditioned on… receipt of payment”</td><td>Pay-if-paid</td><td>Generally unenforceable (<em>Clarke v. Safeco</em>). Ask: “within 7 days after Contractor receives payment, and in no event later than 45 days after billing.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>“retain 10%… until final completion… of the Project”</td><td>Retention waits for the whole project</td><td>5% cap on public and on private contracts from Jan 1, 2026; release at acceptance of your work or after establishment</td></tr>
<tr><td>“unconditional waiver… with each payment application”</td><td>Lien rights gone before you're paid</td><td>Conditional releases with the bill; unconditional after payment clears</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  deeper: `<p>Civil Code §8122: an owner, direct contractor or subcontractor can't, by contract or otherwise, waive or impair another claimant's lien and payment rights; such a term is void unless the claimant signs a statutory waiver and release. The release forms matter more than the contract wording.</p>`,
  src: "Wm. R. Clarke Corp. v. Safeco Ins. Co. (1997) 15 Cal.4th 882; Cal. Civil Code §§8122, 8811; Public Contract Code §7201 (via california.public.law, verified Oct. 2026)."
},

{ h: "Sample clauses · changes, delay, indemnity and the back end",
  lead: "Short notice deadlines, no-damages-for-delay and broad indemnity are where landscape subs lose the most.",
  exLabel: "Sample clauses (made up for teaching)",
  ex: `<p>“No extra work shall be paid unless authorized in advance by a written Change Order signed by Contractor's Project Manager. Subcontractor shall give written notice of any claim within 48 hours of the event giving rise to it, or the claim is waived. Markup on changes shall not exceed 10% for overhead and profit combined.” · “Subcontractor shall perform its Work in accordance with Contractor's schedule as revised from time to time… liable for liquidated damages assessed by Owner to the extent caused by Subcontractor. Subcontractor's sole remedy for delay of any kind shall be an extension of time.”</p>`,
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Phrase</th><th>Effect</th><th>Ask</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>“Change Order signed by… Project Manager”</td><td>Superintendent's word isn't enough</td><td>Add “or written direction from Contractor's superintendent, including signed T&amp;M tickets”</td></tr>
<tr><td>“within 48 hours… or the claim is waived”</td><td>Miss the clock, lose the money</td><td>7 days; calendar whatever you agree; ask the GC its own notice period under the prime</td></tr>
<tr><td>“markup… not exceed 10%”</td><td>Change overhead and profit capped</td><td>Your real markup (<a href="#m10-1">Module 10</a>), separate O&amp;P, a minimum CO amount; “Exhibit B unit prices apply to added or deleted work”</td></tr>
<tr><td>“as revised from time to time”</td><td>GC can squeeze your duration</td><td>Your proposal duration from turnover of ready areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>LDs (liquidated damages) “to the extent caused by Subcontractor”</td><td>Per-day damages for delay you caused</td><td>Keep that phrase; cap at a percent of the subcontract</td></tr>
<tr><td>“sole remedy… extension of time”</td><td>Time, no money for standby or extra trips</td><td>Delete, or “except for delays caused by Contractor or Owner”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indemnity “regardless of fault”</td><td>You pay for others' negligence</td><td>“To the extent caused by Subcontractor's negligence” (Civil Code §§2782, 2782.05)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional insured, primary and non-contributory, waiver of subrogation</td><td>Your policy covers the GC first</td><td>Send to your agent the same day; a COI alone isn't enough</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warranty “one year from final completion of the Project”</td><td>Starts months after you finish</td><td>From acceptance of your work; exclude vandalism, water loss, owner neglect</td></tr>
<tr><td>Termination for convenience</td><td>GC can end it for no reason</td><td>Pay for work done, materials ordered, demobilization, profit on completed work</td></tr>
<tr><td>“arbitration in Orange County”</td><td>Distant forum at your cost</td><td>Mediation first; San Diego County</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  deeper: `<p><strong>PCC §7102:</strong> on public agency construction contracts and their subcontracts, a clause limiting the contractee to a time extension for a delay it caused can't bar damages when the delay is unreasonable and not contemplated by the parties. It doesn't void notice requirements, dispute procedures or LDs. No private-work equivalent: negotiate it.</p>
<p><strong>Civil Code §2782</strong> voids indemnity for a party's sole negligence or willful misconduct and, on public agency contracts, for the agency's active negligence. <strong>§2782.05</strong> (contracts on or after Jan 1, 2013) extends limits to subs on most non-residential private work and lets a sub defend with its own counsel or pay a reasonable share of defense costs; exceptions include wrap-up insurance programs.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Public Contract Code §7102; Civil Code §§2782, 2782.05 (SB 474, 2011) (via california.public.law, verified Oct. 2026)."
},

{ h: "Pay-if-paid and prompt payment deadlines",
  lead: "Pay-when-paid sets timing and is allowed; pay-if-paid makes owner payment a condition and is generally unenforceable in California.",
  body: `<p><em>Wm. R. Clarke Corp. v. Safeco</em> (1997): a true pay-if-paid clause works as an indirect waiver of constitutionally protected mechanics lien rights; a later appellate case applied the rule to public works.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Payment</th><th>Rule</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Late penalty</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Local agency → prime, progress</td><td>PCC §20104.50</td><td>30 days after an undisputed, proper request; improper request returned within 7 days with reasons</td><td>Legal-rate interest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private owner → direct contractor, progress</td><td>Civil Code §8800</td><td>30 days after notice demanding payment, unless otherwise agreed in writing; may hold 150% of a good-faith dispute</td><td>2%/month</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prime/GC → sub, share of progress</td><td>B&amp;P §7108.5</td><td>7 days after receipt, unless otherwise agreed in writing; may hold 150% of a good-faith dispute</td><td>2%/month; license discipline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public agency releases retention</td><td>PCC §7107</td><td>60 days after completion</td><td>2%/month</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prime → sub, share of public retention</td><td>PCC §7107</td><td>7 days after receipt</td><td>2%/month</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private owner releases retention</td><td>Civil Code §8812</td><td>45 days after completion</td><td>2%/month</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private GC → sub, share of retention</td><td>Civil Code §8814</td><td>10 days after receipt; may hold 150% of a good-faith dispute</td><td>2%/month</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>“Unless otherwise agreed in writing” lets subcontracts set longer terms: negotiate them. PCC = Public Contract Code; B&amp;P = Business and Professions Code. Caltrans and other state agencies follow their own payment rules. Read the current section and talk to an attorney before demanding a penalty.</p>`,
  src: "Wm. R. Clarke Corp. v. Safeco Ins. Co. (1997) 15 Cal.4th 882; Cal. Public Contract Code §§7107, 20104.50; Civil Code §§8800, 8812, 8814; Business and Professions Code §7108.5 (via california.public.law, verified Oct. 2026)."
},

{ h: "Retention: caps and release",
  lead: "California generally caps retention at 5% on public work and on private contracts entered on or after Jan 1, 2026.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Job type</th><th>Cap</th><th>Release to GC</th><th>GC pays your share</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Public</strong> (city, school, water district)</td><td>5% of each payment and of the contract, limited exceptions</td><td>60 days after completion</td><td>7 days after receipt</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Private, contracts on or after Jan 1, 2026</strong></td><td>5% (SB 61, Civil Code §8811); excludes residential that isn't mixed-use and is four stories or less</td><td>45 days after completion of the work of improvement</td><td>10 days after receipt</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Caltrans prime</strong></td><td>No retention from progress payments (<a href="#m15-1">Module 15</a>)</td><td>n/a</td><td>Per your subcontract</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>K-24-1952: the City's pay estimate computed retention as 5% of total billed to date, and primes had to return retention to subs within 30 calendar days after the sub's work was satisfactorily completed and accepted, including partial acceptances.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>“Completion” usually means the whole project; landscape finishes last and then runs establishment. Write release at acceptance of your work into the subcontract.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p><strong>Public:</strong> PCC §7201 caps retention at 5% for the agency, the prime and every sub tier; an agency can exceed it only by finding, before bid, that a project is substantially complex. A prime may hold more than 5% from a sub who fails to provide bonds the prime required in writing before bid. AB 2173 made the cap permanent from Jan 1, 2023. PCC §22300 lets the contractor substitute securities or use an escrow for retention and earn the interest; subs doing more than 5% of the bid can ask for the same.</p>
<p><strong>Private:</strong> Civil Code §8811 doesn't apply to a sub who fails to give bonds the contractor required in writing at or before bid request. Check mixed-use jobs with an attorney.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Public Contract Code §§7107, 7201, 22300; AB 2173 (2022); SB 61 (2025), Civil Code §8811; Civil Code §§8812, 8814; City of San Diego K-24-1952 pay estimate form and EOCP Section J."
},

{ h: "Submittals",
  lead: "Nothing is ordered until its submittal is approved; one complete package per spec section gets approved fastest.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Submittal</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Irrigation product data</td><td>Cut sheets for every head, nozzle, valve, backflow, controller, sensor, pipe and wire, exact model marked, unused models crossed out</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plant material</td><td>Nursery sources, sizes, quantities; photos or tagging for specimen and 24" box and larger</td></tr>
<tr><td>Soil, amendments, compost, mulch</td><td>Agronomic test from an approved lab with recommendations; product data, tests, samples</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recycled water items</td><td>Purple pipe, tags, signs per water agency and county rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule and work plans</td><td>Installation schedule; establishment plan if required</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> copy each Division 32 section's (or Caltrans section 20's) submittal list into a log → cover sheet (project, section, submittal # such as 32 84 00-01, items, deviations, dates, signature) → check models and sizes against the plan legend → send per the GC's format by the precon due date → order only on “approved” or “approved as noted”, following the notes → order long-lead items (box trees, controllers, backflow enclosures) the day of approval.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Section</th><th>Item</th><th>Due</th><th>Sent</th><th>Status</th><th>Long lead</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>01</td><td>32 84 00</td><td>Irrigation products</td><td>03/02</td><td>02/26</td><td>Approved as noted</td><td>Controller, 3 wk</td></tr>
<tr><td>03</td><td>32 93 00</td><td>Plant sources, tree photos</td><td>03/16</td><td></td><td>Not sent</td><td>36" box, 6 wk</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Caltrans 11-431064 allowed 5 working days per submittal review and required a plant vendor statement of names, sizes, quantities and expected delivery date. On 11-428704 Caltrans could withhold payment when start-of-work submittals were late.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>“Or equal” products need their own approval; never install a substitute because the specified product was out of stock. A “not sent” beside a long-lead item is a schedule delay.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-431064 Special Provisions (submittal review time; plant vendor statement); 11-428704 Special Provisions."
},

{ h: "Precon, hold points and trade coordination",
  lead: "Leave the precon with names, dates and hold points, and get the early pieces (sleeves, power) in before other trades bury them.",
  body: `<p><strong>Bring:</strong> schedule, submittal log, insurance, questions, emergency contact, foreman's name. <strong>Leave knowing:</strong> who approves changes, signs T&amp;M tickets and schedules inspections; pay app due date, format, recipient; staging, parking, water; sleeve, paving, curb and wall dates; hold points and notice; establishment start and who accepts planting.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Before…</th><th>You must…</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Paving, curbs, sidewalks, walls</td><td>Install sleeves per plan, marked at both ends; verify locations with the inspector at hardscape layout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrical rough-in</td><td>Confirm controller power and conduit are in the electrician's scope and location</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accepting an area</td><td>Walk it; note in writing grade, debris, compaction, damaged sleeves, washout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backfilling trenches</td><td>Pass the pressure test with the inspector present</td></tr>
<tr><td>Planting</td><td>Finish irrigation, test coverage, complete weed cycles</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  exLabel: "Real example: K-24-1952 hold points",
  ex: `<p>The Resident Engineer observed, on 3 working days' notice: precon; protection of existing plants; rough grade and soil tests; plant material; soil prep and finish grade; plant layout and installation; substantial completion punch list; punch list completion (starts the maintenance period); maintenance completion; sleeve locations at hardscape layout; mainline and lateral pressure tests before backfill; coverage check; final walk-through at the end of the 90-day maintenance period. Starting work “constitutes acceptance of the conditions.” Grading, drainage or hardscape handed to you: <a href="#m17-1">Module 17</a>.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, landscape Special Provisions (site observations)."
},

{ h: "Schedule of values",
  lead: "Split the price into lines that follow the work; front-loading gets the SOV (schedule of values) rejected.",
  exLabel: "Example numbers: a $186,400 landscape subcontract",
  body: `<p>Scope: landscape and irrigation per L-1.0 to L-4.2, sections 32 84 00 and 32 93 00, 90-day establishment.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Line</th><th class="num">Value</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Mobilization, bonds, submittals</td><td class="num">$7,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>POC (point of connection), backflow, controller</td><td class="num">$14,200</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Mainline, sleeves, wire</td><td class="num">$22,600</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Laterals, heads, drip</td><td class="num">$38,400</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Soil prep and fine grade</td><td class="num">$16,300</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Trees, 24" box</td><td class="num">$27,600</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Shrubs and groundcover</td><td class="num">$34,800</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Mulch</td><td class="num">$9,700</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>90-day plant establishment</td><td class="num">$12,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>Closeout: as-builts, controller charts, training</td><td class="num">$3,300</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>$186,400</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>An SOV billing 40% in month one gets rejected. Give establishment and closeout real values so you're paid for them and the GC has a reason to sign them off.</p>`
},

{ h: "Pay application #2 with retention",
  lead: "Due now = completed to date − retention − previous payments.",
  exLabel: "Example numbers (continues the $186,400 subcontract)",
  body: `<p>CO #1 (two valves and a sleeve) approved, <strong>$2,850</strong>. Pay app #1 billed $21,060 (line 1 at 100%, line 3 at 60%).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Line</th><th class="num">Value</th><th class="num">% done</th><th class="num">To date</th><th class="num">Previous</th><th class="num">This period</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1 Mobilization</td><td class="num">7,500</td><td class="num">100%</td><td class="num">7,500</td><td class="num">7,500</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 POC, backflow, controller</td><td class="num">14,200</td><td class="num">60%</td><td class="num">8,520</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">8,520</td></tr>
<tr><td>3 Mainline</td><td class="num">22,600</td><td class="num">100%</td><td class="num">22,600</td><td class="num">13,560</td><td class="num">9,040</td></tr>
<tr><td>4 Laterals</td><td class="num">38,400</td><td class="num">75%</td><td class="num">28,800</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">28,800</td></tr>
<tr><td>5 Soil prep</td><td class="num">16,300</td><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num">8,150</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">8,150</td></tr>
<tr><td>6–10</td><td class="num">87,400</td><td class="num">0%</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
<tr><td>CO #1</td><td class="num">2,850</td><td class="num">100%</td><td class="num">2,850</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">2,850</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>189,250</strong></td><td class="num">41.4%</td><td class="num"><strong>78,420</strong></td><td class="num">21,060</td><td class="num"><strong>57,360</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><tbody>
<tr><td>A. Original contract</td><td class="num">$186,400.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>B. Approved change orders</td><td class="num">$2,850.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Contract to date (A + B)</td><td class="num">$189,250.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Completed to date</td><td class="num">$78,420.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>E. Retention, 5% of D</td><td class="num">$3,921.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>F. Previous payments (21,060 − 1,053)</td><td class="num">$20,007.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>G. Due now (D − E − F)</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>$54,492.00</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>H. Balance to finish (C − D)</td><td class="num">$110,830.00</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Check:</strong> 57,360 × 0.95 = $54,492. Still to collect: 110,830 + 3,921 retention = $114,751. At 100%, retention = 5% × 189,250 = $9,462.50.</p>`,
  watch: `<p><strong>Monthly package by the GC's cutoff</strong> (often the 20th–25th): signed pay app and SOV sheet, conditional progress release for this bill, unconditional release for last month only if that check cleared, certified payroll on prevailing wage jobs (<a href="#m11-1">Module 11</a>), only COs approved in writing. Log date sent, amount, date paid, days to pay; a slow-paying GC affects your next go/no-go.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>The A–H layout follows the City of San Diego pay estimate form. Private GCs often use AIA G702/G703 (American Institute of Architects) with the same math.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A pay estimate form (Appendix). Contract amounts are made up for practice."
},

{ h: "Add and deduct change orders through the retention bill",
  lead: "A deduct CO bills as a negative line, retention applies to the net, and total payments must equal the contract to date.",
  exLabel: "Example numbers: a $96,500 park subcontract",
  body: `<p>SOV: mobilization $4,000 · irrigation $38,500 · soil prep $9,000 · planting $33,000 · mulch $6,000 · establishment $6,000 = <strong>$96,500</strong>. CO #1 relocate controller <strong>+$1,850</strong>. CO #2 delete (40) #5 shrubs at the $50 deduct price <strong>−$2,000</strong>. Contract to date = <strong>$96,350</strong>. Pay app #1 billed $19,400, paid $18,430.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Bill</th><th>Complete</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Due</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>#2</td><td>Mob, irrigation, soil prep 100%; planting 60%; both COs</td><td>Completed 4,000 + 38,500 + 9,000 + 19,800 + 1,850 − 2,000 = 71,150; retention 3,557.50; previous 18,430</td><td class="num">$49,162.50</td></tr>
<tr><td>#3</td><td>Planting, mulch 100%</td><td>Completed 90,350; retention 4,517.50; previous 67,592.50</td><td class="num">$18,240.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>#4</td><td>Establishment 100%</td><td>Completed 96,350; retention 4,817.50; previous 85,832.50</td><td class="num">$5,700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retention</td><td>All work accepted</td><td>5% × 96,350</td><td class="num">$4,817.50</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Total:</strong> 18,430 + 49,162.50 + 18,240 + 5,700 + 4,817.50 = <strong>$96,350.00</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Release dates, public (example):</strong> acceptance Mar 2, 2027 → agency releases by May 1 (60 days, PCC §7107) → prime receives it Apr 23, pays your share by Apr 30 (7 days). <strong>Private:</strong> owner 45 days after completion (§8812); GC 10 days after receipt (§8814).</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Take a deduct when approved; an unclaimed deduct found later costs you the GC's trust in all your billing. Send the retention bill with a conditional final release (Civil Code §8136); sign the unconditional final (§8138) only after the check clears.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Public Contract Code §7107; Civil Code §§8136, 8138, 8812, 8814. Contract amounts are made up."
},

{ h: "Lien rights and preliminary notices",
  lead: "California's payment remedies work only if you serve notices on time and act before the deadlines.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Private job</th><th>Public job</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Tools</strong></td><td>Mechanics lien, stop payment notice, payment bond claim (if bonded)</td><td>No lien on public property: stop payment notice to the agency and claim on the prime's payment bond</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Preliminary notice</strong></td><td>Sub serves the 20-day notice on owner, GC and any construction lender</td><td>Required for claimants without a direct contract with the prime; a sub hired directly by the prime is generally exempt for stop payment notices, its suppliers and sub-subs are not</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Timing</strong></td><td colspan="2">Within 20 days of first furnishing. A late notice covers only work from 20 days before service.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Act by</strong></td><td>Record lien 90 days after completion, or 30 days after a recorded NOC (notice of completion) for a sub (60 for a direct contractor); sue within 90 days of recording</td><td>Stop payment notice 30 days after a recorded NOC or acceptance, or 90 days after completion if none recorded</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Serve a preliminary notice on every private job in the first week. Calendar lien and stop payment deadlines the day an NOC is recorded.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>Private: Civil Code §§8200, 8204 (preliminary notice), 8412, 8414 (recording), 8460 (enforcement). Public: §§9300–9304 (preliminary notice), 9356 (stop payment notice timing), 9550 (payment bond on public works over $25,000). Summaries vary; check the current code and an attorney before relying on a deadline for a real claim.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Civil Code §§8200, 8204, 8412, 8414, 8460, 9300–9304, 9356, 9550."
},

{ h: "Lien deadlines · private job worked example",
  lead: "Convert each rule to a calendar date the day the triggering event happens.",
  exLabel: "Example dates (2026)",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Rule</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>First furnish work</td><td></td><td>Mon, Mar 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Serve preliminary notice</td><td>20 days (§8204)</td><td>By Sun, Mar 22 (serve week one)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Work of improvement completed</td><td>§8180</td><td>Fri, Aug 14</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner records NOC</td><td></td><td>Tue, Aug 18</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Last day to record lien (sub)</strong></td><td>30 days after NOC (§8414)</td><td><strong>Thu, Sep 17</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>If no NOC recorded</td><td>90 days after completion (§8414)</td><td>Thu, Nov 12</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lien recorded</td><td></td><td>Thu, Sep 10</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Last day to sue to enforce</strong></td><td>90 days after recording (§8460)</td><td><strong>Wed, Dec 9</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Late notice:</strong> served Mon, Apr 6 → covers work from Tue, Mar 17 on; Mar 2–16 is unprotected. The GC, a direct contractor, gets 60 days after the NOC (§8412); you get 30.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>Completion under §8180 also occurs by occupation or use with cessation of labor, by 60 days of continuous cessation, or by recording a notice of cessation after 30 days. On work subject to public entity acceptance, completion occurs on acceptance.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Civil Code §§8180, 8204, 8412, 8414, 8460 (via california.public.law, verified Oct. 2026)."
},

{ h: "Stop payment and bond deadlines · public job worked example",
  lead: "On public work the backstops are the stop payment notice and the payment bond, each with its own suit deadline.",
  exLabel: "Example dates (2026–2027)",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Rule</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Agency accepts project</td><td>§8180(b)</td><td>Thu, Oct 1, 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agency records NOC</td><td></td><td>Mon, Oct 5</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Last day for stop payment notice</strong></td><td>30 days after recording (§9356)</td><td><strong>Wed, Nov 4</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Notice given</td><td></td><td>Tue, Oct 20</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earliest suit on the notice</td><td>10 days after giving it (§9502)</td><td>Fri, Oct 30</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Last day to sue on the notice</strong></td><td>90 days after the notice period ends (§9502)</td><td><strong>Tue, Feb 2, 2027</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Last day to sue on the bond</strong></td><td>6 months after the notice period ends (§9558)</td><td><strong>Tue, May 4, 2027</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>No NOC recorded: the stop payment deadline is 90 days after cessation or completion (§9356). Ask the agency whether and when it recorded one, and call an attorney well before the last day.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Civil Code §§8180, 9356, 9502, 9558 (via california.public.law, verified Oct. 2026)."
},

{ h: "Lien releases: conditional and unconditional",
  lead: "Never sign an unconditional release before the money has cleared: it takes effect when signed, paid or not.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Civil Code</th><th>When to sign</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Conditional, <strong>progress</strong></td><td>§8132</td><td>With each pay app; effective only when paid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unconditional, <strong>progress</strong></td><td>§8134</td><td>After the progress check clears</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conditional, <strong>final</strong></td><td>§8136</td><td>With the final bill, including retention</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unconditional, <strong>final</strong></td><td>§8138</td><td>After final payment clears</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> the GC's accountant won't release $54,492 without an unconditional progress release. Send the §8132 conditional for the same amount and through-date; list unpaid COs and retention as exceptions; offer the unconditional the day the check clears or in exchange for a cashier's check; if refused, escalate to the GC's PM. Most GCs accept conditional releases once asked.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>A waiver is effective only if substantially in the statutory form and signed. CSLB posts the forms. Progress releases cover work through a stated date and leave retention and disputed extras unpaid.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Civil Code §§8132, 8134, 8136, 8138; CSLB, Conditional and Unconditional Waiver and Release Forms (cslb.ca.gov)."
},

{ h: "Change orders, T&M tickets and the log",
  lead: "Extra work without same-day written notice and a signed ticket is usually free work.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Notice the same day</b>What, where, why it differs from plans, specs or your scope letter.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Price it or work T&amp;M</b>COR (change order request) with labor, material, equipment and markup per the subcontract; if no time, written direction to proceed on T&amp;M.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Daily ticket signed that day</b>Date, location, each worker's name, classification and ST/OT (straight time/overtime) hours, equipment hours, materials with quantities, photo numbers, GC name/title/signature.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Bill only approved COs</b>Track the rest in the log.</div></li>
</ol>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Description</th><th>Notice</th><th>COR</th><th class="num">Requested</th><th>Status</th><th>Billed</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Relocate controller</td><td>03/10</td><td>03/11</td><td class="num">$1,850</td><td>CO #1</td><td>Pay app #2</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Delete (40) #5 shrubs</td><td>(GC)</td><td>03/20</td><td class="num">−$2,000</td><td>CO #2</td><td>Pay app #2</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Remove buried footing, T&amp;M #014</td><td>04/15</td><td>04/17</td><td class="num">$2,960</td><td>Pending</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Added drip zone, new planter</td><td>04/20</td><td>04/21</td><td class="num">$2,078.98</td><td>Pending</td><td></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Approved net −$150; pending <strong>$5,038.98</strong> at risk until signed. Chase anything pending over 30 days.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>The GC's signature confirms the hours and equipment happened, not that the work is extra; entitlement is settled in the COR. On prevailing wage jobs, T&amp;M classifications and hours must match certified payroll. Photograph before, during and after.</p>`
},

{ h: "Pricing a change order request",
  lead: "Price a change line by line from cost, like the bid, and attach sketch, photos and the GC's direction.",
  exLabel: "Example numbers (use your rates and the subcontract's markup)",
  body: `<p><strong>Change:</strong> add 1 drip zone (180 LF tubing), 1 remote control valve, (24) #5 shrubs. 2 workers × 7 h.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Amount</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Labor</td><td>14 h × $62</td><td class="num">$868.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Valve with box and kit</td><td>1 × $185</td><td class="num">$185.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drip tubing</td><td>180 LF × $0.45</td><td class="num">$81.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Filter, regulator, fittings</td><td>LS</td><td class="num">$95.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Valve wire</td><td>120 LF × $0.30</td><td class="num">$36.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>#5 shrubs</td><td>24 × $14</td><td class="num">$336.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sales tax on material</td><td>$733.00 × 7.75% (example)</td><td class="num">$56.81</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trencher, half day</td><td>Rental</td><td class="num">$150.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Subtotal</strong></td><td></td><td class="num"><strong>$1,807.81</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Markup</td><td>15% (or the subcontract's)</td><td class="num">$271.17</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>COR total</strong></td><td></td><td class="num"><strong>$2,078.98</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>At your proposal's $78 shrub add price, 24 shrubs alone = $1,872. Use the method the subcontract specifies; don't mix unit prices and cost pricing on the same items.</p>`
},

{ h: "Plant establishment",
  lead: "Run establishment as its own small job: written start date, a weekly checklist, a monthly report, and costs tracked against the bid.",
  body: `<p><strong>Start date in writing:</strong> on K-24-1952 the 90-calendar-day period began with acceptance of the planting.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Weekly check</th><th>Record</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Controller on the seasonal program, no alarms, flow normal</td><td>Program and changes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Run each zone: broken heads, leaks, clogged drip, coverage</td><td>Zones repaired, parts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plant health, dead or missing plants</td><td>Count by species and size; replace same species and size</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weeds, mulch and DG depth, tree stakes and basins, trash</td><td>Areas done, products used</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damage by others (vandalism, vehicles, trades)</td><td>Photos and same-day email to the GC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fixed-spot photos (monthly); crew hours</td><td>Photo numbers; establishment cost code</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Monthly report</strong> (one page to the GC or inspector): visits and crew hours, watering changes, repairs, replacements, weed and fertilizer work, open issues, next steps (request the final walk-through before the period ends), attached logs and photos.</p>`,
  exLabel: "Scenario: inspector rejects (30) #1 plants in month 2",
  ex: `<p>Walk and flag them with the inspector; check logs for cause. Your care → replace at your cost, about 30 × ($4.50 plant + $0.35 tax + 0.25 h × $62) = $610.50. Damage by others or water or power loss → logs, photos, written notice, replace, COR at the $38 add price = $1,140. Ask in writing whether replacements extend the period.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Without weekly logs every dead plant is presumed yours. Caltrans 11-428704 required a 250-working-day Type 1 period, four Calsense seasonal schedules, fertilizer the first week of April, July and September, and an annual irrigation test; bids ran $10,000–$300,000 (<a href="#m15-1">Module 15</a>).</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, Special Provisions 6-1.1; Caltrans 11-428704 Special Provisions 20-4."
},

{ h: "Punch list, closeout and retention",
  lead: "Close the punch list in one round and start closeout paperwork the day planting starts; both gate your retention.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Self-punch</b>Heads, tilt, coverage, mulch, dead plants, debris, before the GC or inspector walks.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Request inspection</b>With the notice the specs require (3 working days on K-24-1952).</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Punch list in writing, closed with photos</b>Then written acceptance: its date usually starts establishment.</div></li>
</ol>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Before billing retention</th><th>Done when</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Punch list and acceptance</td><td>Every item signed off; acceptance date in writing</td></tr>
<tr><td>As-builts</td><td>Mainline, valves, splices, sleeves, changes; marked daily on a field set</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controller charts</td><td>Color-coded zone maps, sealed or laminated, in each controller if specified</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warranties, O&amp;M (operation and maintenance) manuals, spare parts and keys</td><td>Delivered per spec</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner controller training</td><td>Done and signed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change orders</td><td>All approved and billed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Releases and deadlines</td><td>Conditional final (§8136) with the bill; unconditional (§8138) held; NOC checked and lien or stop payment deadline calendared</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>On K-24-1952 the substantial completion punch list and punch list completion each had 7 days in the City's inspection table, and completion authorized the maintenance period. A slow punch list delays establishment, completion and retention.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Warranty callbacks: respond within a day, fix, log the cost. Recurring callbacks on one GC's jobs point to a cause (their maintenance, your installation).</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, landscape Special Provisions (site observations)."
},

{ h: "Job cost review",
  lead: "Compare actual to estimate by the same lines you priced, and change a rate only on repeated results or a named condition.",
  exLabel: "Example numbers: the $96,350 park subcontract",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th class="num">Qty</th><th class="num">Est. rate</th><th class="num">Est. h</th><th class="num">Actual h</th><th class="num">Actual rate</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Laterals, trench and pipe</td><td class="num">3,600 LF</td><td class="num">0.040 h/LF</td><td class="num">144</td><td class="num">126</td><td class="num">0.035</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spray heads</td><td class="num">140 EA</td><td class="num">0.30 h/EA</td><td class="num">42</td><td class="num">49</td><td class="num">0.35</td></tr>
<tr><td>#5 shrubs</td><td class="num">640 EA</td><td class="num">0.25 h/EA</td><td class="num">160</td><td class="num">192</td><td class="num">0.30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mulch</td><td class="num">85 CY</td><td class="num">0.80 h/CY</td><td class="num">68</td><td class="num">60</td><td class="num">0.71</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td class="num"><strong>414</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>427</strong></td><td></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Labor: +13 h (3.1%) × $62 = $806. Mulch: quoted $34/CY, paid $37: 85 × $3 = $255. <strong>Overrun $1,061.</strong></p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Shrubs: 0.25 h/EA normal soil, 0.30 clay or rocky</td><td>Three prior jobs averaged 0.25; this one was hard soil. Named condition, so split the rate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laterals: hold 0.040 for two more jobs</td><td>New trencher; one data point</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mulch: 30-day quote validity or escalation language (<a href="#m13-1">Module 13</a>)</td><td>Supplier raised price mid-job</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Use 5–10 activity codes matching estimate lines, or the foreman guesses. Update the production rate table (<a href="#m9-1">Module 9</a>) after every job with the conditions noted.</p>`,
  src: "Example numbers continue the made-up $96,350 park subcontract."
},

{ h: "Bid review meeting and KPIs",
  lead: "Every two weeks, same agenda, written decisions; monthly, a few KPIs (key performance indicators) show whether bidding works.",
  body: `<p><strong>Biweekly, 45–60 min, owner + estimator + field lead:</strong> open bids and addenda · go/no-go on new invitations (<a href="#m3-1">Module 3</a> scorecard) · results and gap to low · active job cost, pending COs, unpaid pay apps · one pricing change, written · GCs to call. End with who/what/when; open the next meeting by checking it.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>KPI</th><th>Formula</th><th class="num">Months 1–6</th><th class="num">Months 7–12</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bid volume</td><td>Bids per month</td><td class="num">28 ÷ 6 = 4.7</td><td class="num">33 ÷ 6 = 5.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>Win rate, count</td><td>Won ÷ bid</td><td class="num">4 ÷ 28 = 14.3%</td><td class="num">6 ÷ 33 = 18.2%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Win rate, dollars</td><td>$ won ÷ $ bid</td><td class="num">510K ÷ 3,920K = 13.0%</td><td class="num">771K ÷ 4,860K = 15.9%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gap to low (lost bids)</td><td>(Yours − low) ÷ low</td><td class="num">e.g. (212,000 − 193,500) ÷ 193,500 = 9.6%</td><td class="num">Monthly avg 11.0% → 6.4%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Margin, won jobs</td><td>Bid vs actual after job cost</td><td class="num">18% / 14%</td><td class="num"></td></tr>
<tr><td>Repeat GCs</td><td>Wins from GCs who invited before</td><td class="num">3 of 4</td><td class="num"></td></tr>
<tr><td>Backlog</td><td>Won, unbilled</td><td class="num"></td><td class="num">210K → 410K</td></tr>
<tr><td>Days to get paid</td><td>Average per pay app</td><td class="num"></td><td class="num">35–44</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Reading:</strong> actual margin under bid margin → fix production rates before chasing volume. Low win rate, small gap → keep bidding; large gap → recheck costs and markup (<a href="#m10-1">Module 10</a>). Rising backlog ($410K) → confirm crews can carry it, or slow bidding or add a crew.</p>`
},

{ h: "Growing capacity: GCs, people and bonding",
  lead: "Grow in survivable steps: relationships, field and estimating capacity, then bonding for bigger and public work.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>GCs:</strong> build a handful who invite you to everything and give feedback; perform, bill cleanly, answer callbacks; ask yearly, “How can we be easier to work with?”</li>
<li><strong>Field:</strong> don't win more than your foremen can run; add the crew before a job slips.</li>
<li><strong>Estimator hire:</strong> signs are declined invitations, late or unchecked bids, an estimator also running jobs, healthy win rate with stalled volume. Math (example): $95,000 cost ÷ $12,000 gross profit per win = 7.9 → about 8 more wins/year → at 18% win rate, 8 ÷ 0.18 ≈ 44 more bids/year. Cheaper first steps: part-time takeoff help, an office person for forms and uploads, AI drafting with every number checked.</li>
<li><strong>Bonding:</strong> the surety sets single-job and aggregate limits from CPA-prepared year-end statements (on time), working capital, net worth, a quarterly WIP (work in progress) schedule, track record and an unused bank line.</li>
</ul>`,
  exLabel: "Working capital (made-up numbers)",
  ex: `<p>Current assets 260,000 cash + 480,000 receivables + 80,000 retention = $820,000. Current liabilities 330,000 payables + 180,000 short-term debt = $510,000. <strong>Working capital $310,000.</strong> Ask your surety how it converts working capital and equity into limits; methods differ.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p><strong>Before a first public prime:</strong> DIR registration and certified payroll working (<a href="#m11-1">Module 11</a>); performance and payment bonding at the job size; cash for 60–90 days of costs; a traffic control sub for street work; a few agency jobs done as a sub. A job like 11-428704 (about $1.2M, C-27 as prime) is realistic with bonding in place.</p>`
},

{ h: "12-month and 3-year roadmaps",
  lead: "Example targets to adjust quarterly in the bid review; change them when the KPIs say so, not when a big invitation looks exciting.",
  exLabel: "Example targets: adjust to your company",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Focus</th><th>Targets</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Months 1–3</td><td>Systems and relationships</td><td>Tracker, templates, subcontract checklist · 20 GCs contacted, 10 prequalified · portals registered · 3–5 private sub bids/month · biweekly reviews</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 4–6</td><td>First wins, done right</td><td>1–3 jobs built · job cost on every job · clean pay apps and releases · rates updated · sub prices to primes on agency jobs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 7–9</td><td>Public work as a sub</td><td>First prevailing wage job · surety review · monthly KPIs · 5+ repeat GC invitations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 10–12</td><td>Step up</td><td>First small public prime if bonding allows · price one Caltrans job in full · year review · year-2 staffing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Year 2</td><td>Repeat and prove</td><td>5–8 core GCs · 2–4 public jobs as sub · 1–2 small public primes · second foreman · part-time estimating help · surety review twice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Step up in size</td><td>First Caltrans or larger agency prime within bonding · second estimator if the math works · DBE/SLBE (Small Local Business Enterprise) certification decisions · establishment and maintenance contracts as steady income</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Guardrails:</strong> actual margin within a few points of bid; days to pay not rising; backlog the crews can handle; no job over about twice your largest finished job without the owner's sign-off (a course rule of thumb, not a surety rule).</p>`
},

{ t: "terms", h: "Words to know",
  lead: "Full list: <a href=\"#dict\">Bid Dictionary</a>.",
  terms: [
    ["LOI (letter of intent)", "A GC's written notice that it plans to subcontract with you; not a full contract."],
    ["Exhibit", "A document attached to and made part of a contract, such as your proposal."],
    ["Flow-down clause", "Binds you to the GC the way the GC is bound to the owner."],
    ["Rider", "A one-page list of changes you request to a subcontract."],
    ["Pay-when-paid / pay-if-paid", "Timing of your payment after the GC is paid / owner payment as a condition (generally unenforceable in California)."],
    ["Retention", "Money held from each payment until completion; generally capped at 5% in California."],
    ["No damages for delay", "A clause limiting you to more time, no money, when delayed."],
    ["LDs (liquidated damages)", "A set dollar amount per late day."],
    ["Indemnity", "A promise to defend and pay for certain claims."],
    ["Additional insured", "An endorsement extending your policy to the GC or owner."],
    ["Submittal", "Product data, samples or tests sent for approval before ordering."],
    ["SOV (schedule of values)", "The contract price split into billing lines."],
    ["Pay application", "Monthly bill showing percent complete by SOV line."],
    ["AIA G702/G703", "Common private-work pay application forms."],
    ["Preliminary notice", "The 20-day notice preserving lien, stop payment and bond rights."],
    ["Mechanics lien", "A claim against private property for unpaid work."],
    ["NOC (notice of completion)", "A recorded notice that shortens lien and stop payment deadlines."],
    ["Stop payment notice", "Makes the owner or agency hold funds for an unpaid claimant."],
    ["Payment bond claim", "A claim against the surety bond guaranteeing payment to subs and suppliers."],
    ["Conditional / unconditional release", "Lien release effective only on payment / effective when signed."],
    ["CO / COR", "Approved change order / your priced change order request."],
    ["T&amp;M ticket", "Daily record of time and materials on extra work, signed by the GC."],
    ["Punch list", "Items to fix before acceptance."],
    ["Working capital", "Current assets minus current liabilities; a key surety number."],
    ["WIP schedule", "Quarterly list of open jobs: contract, cost, billings, expected profit."]
  ]
},

{ t: "quiz", id: "m16", h: "Check yourself",
  lead: "Apply the rules to the numbers.",
  qs: [
    { q: "A GC emails “you're low, subcontract to follow.” The nursery offers a 5% discount on 36\" box trees if ordered today. What do you do?", a: ["Order to lock the discount", "Order only after a signed subcontract or written authorization covering the work and price", "Order half the trees"], c: 1, why: "An email isn't a contract; without written authority you own the trees if the deal falls through." },
    { q: "Pay app: completed to date $78,420, previous payments $20,007, 5% retention. Payment due?", a: ["$58,413.00", "$54,492.00", "$57,360.00"], c: 1, why: "78,420 − 3,921 − 20,007 = $54,492." },
    { q: "Contract $96,500, CO #1 +$1,850, CO #2 −$2,000. Completed to date $71,150, previous payments $18,430, 5% retention. Payment due?", a: ["$49,162.50", "$52,720.00", "$51,750.00"], c: 0, why: "71,150 − 3,557.50 − 18,430 = $49,162.50." },
    { q: "Private job: the owner records a notice of completion Aug 18. You're a sub. Last day to record your lien?", a: ["Sep 17 (30 days)", "Oct 17 (60 days)", "Nov 16 (90 days)"], c: 0, why: "Civil Code §8414 gives a claimant other than the direct contractor 30 days after a recorded NOC; 60 days is the direct contractor's." },
    { q: "You first furnished work Mar 2 but served the preliminary notice Apr 6. Which work is protected?", a: ["All work from Mar 2", "Only work from Mar 17 on", "None"], c: 1, why: "A late notice covers work from 20 days before service." },
    { q: "Public job, NOC recorded Oct 5, stop payment notice given Oct 20. Last day to sue on the payment bond?", a: ["Feb 2, 2027 (90 days after the notice period)", "May 4, 2027 (6 months after the notice period ends Nov 4)", "Oct 5, 2027"], c: 1, why: "§9558: 6 months after the 30-day notice period ends. Feb 2 is the suit deadline on the stop payment notice (§9502)." },
    { q: "A private GC receives your share of retention from the owner on June 1. Under Civil Code §8814 it must pay you by:", a: ["June 8", "June 11", "July 16"], c: 1, why: "§8814: 10 days after receipt. Seven days is the public rule (PCC §7107)." },
    { q: "The GC's accountant will release $54,492 only against an unconditional progress release. Best response?", a: ["Sign it; the GC is reliable", "Send the §8132 conditional release, list exceptions, and offer the unconditional when the check clears", "Refuse all releases"], c: 1, why: "An unconditional release (§8134) takes effect when signed, paid or not." },
    { q: "Subcontract on a private job: “sole remedy for delay of any kind shall be an extension of time.” What do you ask for?", a: ["Nothing; PCC §7102 protects you", "Delete it or add “except for delays caused by Contractor or Owner”", "Ask for double the time"], c: 1, why: "PCC §7102 applies only to public agency contracts and their subs; on private work you must negotiate it." },
    { q: "#5 shrubs ran 0.30 h/EA on a hard-clay job vs a 0.25 average on three prior jobs. Best update?", a: ["Change the rate to 0.30 for all jobs", "Keep 0.25 for normal soil and add 0.30 for clay or rocky sites", "Ignore it"], c: 1, why: "Change a rate on repeated results or a named condition; here the condition is known." }
  ]
},

{ t: "practice", id: "m16", h: "Practice assignments",
  lead: "Build the tools you'll use on the first job you win.",
  items: [
    { h: "Mark up a real subcontract", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Get one GC's standard subcontract. For each clause: real wording, effect, and OK / ask to change / deal-breaker. Flag pay-if-paid, retention over 5%, broad indemnity, no damages for delay and short notice deadlines. Turn the top asks into a one-page rider for the owner or an attorney.</p>" },
    { h: "Rebuild the pay applications", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>In a spreadsheet, rebuild the $186,400 SOV and pay app #2 to get $54,492.00 due, then pay app #3 (lines 2, 4, 5 at 100%, line 6 at 50%, line 7 at 25%). Rebuild the $96,350 example through the retention bill and confirm total payments equal the contract.</p>" },
    { h: "Release forms and a deadline calendar", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Download the four statutory releases from the <a href=\"https://cslb.ca.gov/consumers/legal_issues_for_consumers/Mechanics_Lien/Conditional_And_Unconditional_Waiver_Release_Form.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CSLB waiver and release page</a> and fill in a conditional progress release for pay app #2. Decide how preliminary notices will be sent (in-house or a service). For a real or current job, build the lien or stop payment deadline table on a real calendar.</p>" },
    { h: "Submittal log and irrigation package", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>From a real landscape spec (Division 32, or Caltrans 11-428704 or 11-431064 section 20 in the <a href=\"#lab\">Real Bid Lab</a>), log every required submittal with section, contents, due date and long-lead flag. Build the complete irrigation products package: cover sheet plus marked cut sheets.</p>" },
    { h: "COR, T&M ticket and CO log", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>With the company's real labor rate and a real supplier price list, price a change (e.g. 200 SF of planting, 30 #5 shrubs, one drip zone) line by line. Fill in a T&amp;M ticket for a day of extra work and start the CO log.</p>" },
    { h: "Job cost and KPI page", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Set 5–10 activity codes matching your estimate lines and a daily time card. Review a recent job's estimated vs actual hours and write one rate decision. From the <a href=\"https://claude.ai/artifact/BfM3VhU69VAesNWb6kbEnP\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Commercial Bid Tracker</a>, build the month-by-month KPI table (use Real Bid Lab results if data is thin).</p>" },
    { h: "Roadmap and first bid review", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>Adapt the 12-month and 3-year roadmaps with real targets and guardrails. Ask your surety agent what would raise your limits and add it. Write the next bid review agenda and bring it all to the owner.</p>" }
  ]
},

{ h: "Module 16 summary",
  lead: "From award to final payment, then each job feeding the next bid.",
  body: `<ul>
<li>Low isn't a contract. Review every clause, attach your proposal as an exhibit, send a one-page rider.</li>
<li>Pay-if-paid is generally unenforceable; prompt payment runs on 7-, 10-, 30-, 45- and 60-day clocks; retention is generally capped at 5%.</li>
<li>Complete submittals with a log; leave precon with names, dates and hold points; sleeves before paving.</li>
<li>Bill from a fair SOV with the A–H math; deducts are negative lines; payments must sum to the contract.</li>
<li>Serve preliminary notices in week one, calendar lien, stop payment and bond deadlines as dates, use conditional releases until money clears.</li>
<li>Same-day notice, signed T&amp;M tickets, CORs priced from cost, a CO log.</li>
<li>Run establishment with weekly logs and monthly reports; close out with a checklist so retention comes back.</li>
<li>Change rates on evidence, track KPIs monthly, grow bonding and staff in yearly steps.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Next:</strong> <a href="#m17-1">Module 17</a> covers the other scopes in landscape bids; <a href="#m18-1">Module 18</a> is the capstone Caltrans bid. Keep pricing in the <a href="#lab">Real Bid Lab</a>.</p>`
}
]
});
